{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_57","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc 'git diff 8e3009b2 22a03dce -- POSE.md locales/pt-BR/POSE.md .pose/templates/roadmap.md .pose/indexes/validation-matrix.json'","aggregated_output":"diff --git a/.pose/indexes/validation-matrix.json b/.pose/indexes/validation-matrix.json\nindex ae572293..c76f670b 100644\n--- a/.pose/indexes/validation-matrix.json\n+++ b/.pose/indexes/validation-matrix.json\n@@ -311,10 +311,23 @@\n       \"stack\": \"go\",\n       \"replaceDefaultChecks\": true,\n       \"checks\": [\n+        {\n+          \"name\": \"claude-sandboxed-review-integration\",\n+          \"program\": \"go\",\n+          \"args\": [\n+            \"test\",\n+            \"./internal/pose\",\n+            \"-run\",\n+            \"TestSandboxedReview\",\n+            \"-count=1\"\n+          ],\n+          \"evidenceClass\": \"integration\",\n+          \"severity\": \"required\"\n+        },\n         {\n           \"name\": \"delegated-output-redaction-diagnostics-integration\",\n           \"program\": \"go\",\n-          \"args\": [\"test\", \"./internal/pose\", \"./internal/cli\", \"-run\", \"TestDelegateTaskExplainsTheLegacyClaudeSessionCaptureFailure|TestDelegationCaptureFailureWithholdsPrivateErrorDetails\", \"-count=1\"],\n+          \"args\": [\"test\", \"./internal/pose\", \"./internal/cli\", \"-run\", \"TestDelegateTaskExplainsTheLegacyClaudeSessionCaptureFailure|TestDelegateTaskDiagnosesRedactionGrowth|TestDelegationCaptureFailureWithholdsPrivateErrorDetails\", \"-count=1\"],\n           \"severity\": \"required\",\n           \"evidenceClass\": \"integration\"\n         },\n@@ -328,8 +341,7 @@\n         {\n           \"name\": \"test\",\n           \"program\": \"go\",\n-          \"args\": [\"test\", \"./...\", \"-count=1\", \"-timeout=30m\"],\n-          \"timeoutSeconds\": 1800,\n+          \"args\": [\"test\", \"./...\", \"-count=1\"],\n           \"severity\": \"required\",\n           \"evidenceClass\": \"unit\"\n         },\n@@ -2351,6 +2363,13 @@\n           \"severity\": \"required\",\n           \"evidenceClass\": \"integration\"\n         },\n+        {\n+          \"name\": \"closeout-review-policy-integration\",\n+          \"program\": \"go\",\n+          \"args\": [\"test\", \"./internal/pose\", \"./internal/cli\", \"./internal/mcpserver\", \"-run\", \"^TestCloseoutReviewPolicy\", \"-count=1\"],\n+          \"severity\": \"required\",\n+          \"evidenceClass\": \"integration\"\n+        },\n         {\n           \"name\": \"milestone-closes-when-reached-integration\",\n           \"program\": \"go\",\ndiff --git a/.pose/templates/roadmap.md b/.pose/templates/roadmap.md\nindex 54699c8b..a234d003 100644\n--- a/.pose/templates/roadmap.md\n+++ b/.pose/templates/roadmap.md\n@@ -3,6 +3,7 @@ slug: <roadmap-slug>\n status: draft        # draft | active | done | abandoned\n created_at: <YYYY-MM-DD>\n depends_on:          # prerequisite roadmaps, inline list: other-roadmap-a, other-roadmap-b\n+adrs:                # optional ADR associations, inline list: [adr:<file>.md] (closeout review policy, when-justified)\n ---\n \n # Roadmap: <roadmap-slug>\ndiff --git a/POSE.md b/POSE.md\nindex 404ee8cb..1f1d376e 100644\n--- a/POSE.md\n+++ b/POSE.md\n@@ -371,6 +371,7 @@ pose release-package-manifests --version X.Y.Z --checksums <checksums.txt> --out\n   **Raw history:** every history reader of structural review and of the delivery graph ignores replace refs and grafts; a repository with either gets other review inputs than before, so rerun `pose index` and reseal. A trailer change set that starts at the repository's first commit resolves against the empty tree.\n   **Removal witnesses:** a review subject path a later spec removed reads as that removal only while Git still shows it: the deleting commit on `HEAD`, read raw, with no later commit touching the path, in a clone that is not shallow. Otherwise preparation refuses and asks for `pose index`. A generation binds the change sets of `.pose/reports/history` as `recorded-change-sets`, so one published earlier reads as unbound until the next `pose index`.\n   **Children:** a milestone of one local member has no review of its own and stands on that spec's closed approval, and a roadmap may declare `unreviewed_milestones` for milestones whose members are all terminal, sealed by its own review. Otherwise a milestone or roadmap's approval stands only while the review of each child does: a child rejected, stale, with lost attestation history, or not closed under a done parent stops the parent verifying approved, in `review verify`, `review-check` and closeout, and the parent recovers with its child, with no reseal. Selection follows replacement lineage: a replacement of the same content comes before what it replaces whatever the seal times say, a `replaces` naming other content counts for nothing, and a replaced bundle's approval is not retained.\n+  **Closeout review policy (schema 5):** an instance that adopts `pose adopt closeout-review-policy` decides with `milestone_review_required` and `roadmap_review_required` (`never`, `when-justified`, `always`) whether the close of a milestone or a roadmap owes its own review. `when-justified` requires it for a milestone with more than two distinct specs, and for a roadmap with more than one distinct spec and at least one ADR association (`adrs: [adr:<file>.md]` on the roadmap, `roadmaps: [roadmap:<slug>]` on the ADR, or a local Markdown link either way). `always` requires it even for a one-member milestone, and neither the singleton inheritance nor `unreviewed_milestones` weakens it. Member reviews stay required; a review recorded on the scope anyway is verified as before and its rejection stands; a member or association that cannot be resolved never grants an exemption. A milestone that closes without its own review keeps its exit gate: the gate is carried into the review plan of its only member, or of its last member (the one sealed once every other member is terminal), and the close is blocked until an approved member review judged the gate as it reads now, or the milestone is reviewed itself. A member a transfer moved counts at its destination, and a local milestone or roadmap named by `after:` or `consumes:` satisfies the edge only once closed, not by its members' status. Under the adopted policy `pose close milestone:<roadmap>/<id>` and `pose close roadmap:<slug>` stay explicit in every mode and publish a receipt (`review-closeouts/<milestone|roadmap>/…json` inside `.pose/`) with `review: required|not-required`, the key, the value, `closeout-review@1` and the reason (`members=1, threshold>2`); the scope is terminal only with it. New instances adopt `never`/`never`; an existing policy keeps the legacy rule until it adopts, `pose setup` shows both, `pose doctor` lists open scopes that owe a review, and roadmaps closed before adoption are verified by the contract they were sealed under.\n - `close` — applies a review-gated lifecycle transition to complete a spec, milestone, or roadmap. The transition is published only over the bytes its gates judged, under the spec's lifecycle lock that start and transfer also take (spec `pose-writer-coordination-and-atomic-publication`): an edit made while the gates ran is a `publish-conflict` that keeps the edit, the authorization is judged again inside the lock, and a start or transfer holding the spec refuses the close with the lock's owner (`start-in-progress` or `spec-lifecycle-in-progress`). Git state (spec `pose-git-and-dispatch-snapshot-integrity`): a `git status`, HEAD or tree that cannot be read is unknown, not clean — the close plan blocks on it rather than sealing evidence over a working tree it could not see (a directory with no repository at all is a different fact and keeps its behaviour); the governed snapshot, the review-bundle path check and the obligation snapshot report an unreadable status as unknown too. `review dispatch` compares the review worktree with the sealed commit's own tree before and after the run, so `assume-unchanged`, `skip-worktree`, a replaced type or a link cannot hide an edit; native derived regeneration requires exact producer outputs that remain regular files; adapter output belongs on stdout or the external output path, and directory names grant no permission to modify the review worktree, and a run that finishes after its bundle was superseded or its policy changed is recorded `stale`, history and not the scope's current fulfilment.\n - `continuous-closeout` — persists and projects continuous closeout terminal state for rapid-iteration workflows. `status --json` writes one operation result whose `data` holds `active` and `terminal`; the exit stays 1 while an active scope is not terminal.\n - **Output channels** — the result (verdict, findings, data) goes to stdout and progress (steps, usage, the command's own failures) to stderr, so `pose x --json | jq` is safe and `2>/dev/null` never hides a finding. `--json` prints one document whose finding `severity` is the untranslated anchor; `--quiet` prints the verdict alone; `pose validate --verbose` streams a check's output instead of capturing it. Colour, symbols and the spinner exist only on a terminal, resolved per stream, and honour `--color`, `POSE_COLOR`, `NO_COLOR` and `TERM`; `COLUMNS` sets the wrap width and `POSE_LOCALE` (`en` or `pt-BR`) the language of every message POSE composes. Exit codes: 0 pass, 1 gate failure, 2 usage error.\n@@ -899,6 +900,10 @@ versus task and follow native next actions.\n \n `pose review` governs the subject (bundle, brief, attestation, verify); `pose delegate` governs executions. An **agent** is a CLI profile configured in `.pose/policy/delegation.json`; a **run** is one execution; an **attempt** is a run and its continuations; a **thread** is the rounds of one scope. `pose delegate review`, `adjudicate` and `smoke` run the sealed bundle's generated brief on a disposable worktree at the sealed commit with read access only, under the attempt ledger and containment below; any other `--access` is refused. The agent never records an attestation: the engine records the conclusion of a completed review run only as the conclusions of delegated reviews below describe. Without `--agent` (or with `auto`) routing chooses among the agents of the purpose: first by `routing.prefer` (default `opposite-vendor-of-implementation`, in the order the policy declares its agents), then by the steps of `routing.on_unavailable` (default `same-vendor-other-model`, `wait`, `same-model`). `wait` applies only when every earlier candidate is limited with a known reset within `routing.max_wait` (default 5h) and starts the run after the first reset, without polling. The implementation routed against is the identity the bundle sealed; for a bundle sealed before bundles recorded it, the policy's `provenance.implementation`, else `--lead`. An agent named with `--agent` bypasses routing and warns when it shares the implementation's vendor. Every run records the decision (`routing`: the candidates, why each was skipped, the step taken) and `independence_achieved` (`different-vendor`, `different-model` or `same-model`); with no agent available the run does not start and the error carries the decision. `--label` names the run, `--lead <vendor>[/<model>]` declares the implementation when the bundle records none, `--dry-run` prints the resolved agent, purpose, access, working directory and argv (authorized credential values redacted) and reserves nothing, and `--json` prints one versioned document (`pose.delegate.run/v1`, `plan/v1`, `ls/v1`, `cancel/v1`, `agents/v1`, `error/v1`), usage errors included. On `ls`, `resume`, `cancel` and `agents` the same flags select: a run or an agent that does not match is not listed, or is refused when named; `--dry-run` changes nothing anywhere. Messages follow `POSE_LOCALE` (`en` or `pt-BR`). A run reference is a run id, an attempt id, a label or a session prefix of six characters or more; an ambiguous reference exits 2 and lists the candidates.\n \n+A schema-2 agent may opt in to `review_profile.command` and `review_profile.resume_command` for governed review only. Omission preserves its existing read adapter; tasks, adjudication and smoke keep their commands. A resumable agent must declare the profile's resumed command with `{session}`. Once selected, failure never falls back to the read or task profile. The selected commands remain bound by policy/provenance verification. Older strict parsers reject the new field. The configured Claude event codec canonicalizes repeated typed init records only for the same parsed session identity before session recognition. Different identities and generic regex ambiguity are still refused; raw provenance/redaction and limit/reset channels retain the original bytes.\n+\n+This repository's Claude review profile uses safe/restricted mode, Read/Grep/Glob/Bash only, mandatory shell sandboxing (`failIfUnavailable:true`, `allowUnsandboxedCommands:false`), and a read-only sealed source tree. Both fresh and resumed wrappers require bubblewrap, socat and jq on Linux (jq remains required on macOS), with installation advice on refusal. Go cache/temp writes go to private operation-owned scratch outside the checkout; the existing module cache is read, and unavailable downloads/tests that write into source can fail. The profile instructs the reviewer to run `go clean -cache` as its final Bash command inside the sandbox; the engine independently retains incomplete cleanup as a failed run under its unchanged limits. Never clean an adapter-controlled cache outside the sandbox: a test can replace its path with a symlink. This qualifies Claude's shell subprocess boundary, not isolation of the trusted Claude executable or arbitrary credential reads. Checkout integrity checks still apply. Installing engine code does not adopt the profile in another instance; copy and qualify its explicit policy block before use. The wrapper also denies the resolved physical Git common directory, because Claude auto-allows it for worktrees. For this opt-in profile, POSE prepares only empty `.claude/.cc-writes` directories at the disposable checkout root and declared components before taking its integrity snapshot; added files and all later mutations still fail. Final sandboxed cleanup includes leftover `go-build*` directories directly under GOTMPDIR after all jobs finish.\n+\n `pose delegate ls` lists attempts by their latest run with state, purpose, agent, label, turns, age and last step — for an interrupted run also its cause, whether a partial draft exists, the reset instant and the time left — and the runs in progress with the liveness of their process. `pose delegate cancel <run>` stops a run in progress; it is recorded `failed` with the cause `cancelled`, and its attempt stays consumed. `pose delegate agents` lists each agent with vendor, model, whether its CLI is on the path, the reset instant of its last recorded usage limit and the state routing reads: `available`, `limited until <instant>` (its last run hit a usage limit whose reset is ahead), `missing` (its CLI is not on the path), `unknown` (the run history could not be read whole) or `probe-failed` (its optional `probe` failed within its bound). Availability is read from the path and the recorded runs; only a configured probe runs anything. `pose delegate review|adjudicate|smoke --detach` starts the run as a process of its own, in a session of its own that survives the caller's terminal, and returns at once with the attempt (a run reference) and the path of its log; a run whose route must first wait for a usage-limit reset is not detached. `pose delegate wait <run> [--timeout <d>] [--json]` is a plain foreground process that ends when the run does and prints one line — run, state, decision, resumable flag, reset instant — with the exit code `0` approved, `10` changes requested or rejected, `20` failed (a completed run with no decision too), `30` interrupted, `40` stalled, `50` the wait timed out, and `2` for a usage error; every waiter is released, a run already ended returns at once, and killing a wait leaves the run running. The process running an attempt rewrites a heartbeat in its marker every 10 seconds; a run whose heartbeat is older than three intervals and whose process is provably gone is recorded `stalled` by the first reader, through an exclusive claim every reader then agrees on, and `ls` shows each running run's heartbeat age. `pose delegate watch [<run>|<label>]` shows an attempt as one task: the runs it recorded are replayed with a marker where a run resumed another and the interrupted run naming the run that continued it, then a running attempt is followed through the normalized, redacted events its process writes as they come, until the record is published; `--no-follow` prints what exists and exits, `-v` adds the tail of each tool's output (`--lines <n>`, default 12) and `--json` prints NDJSON. With no reference it follows the only running run; at a terminal it otherwise opens fzf with a preview of the run under the cursor refreshed every `--refresh <d>` (default 2s, 0 turns it off), or a numbered menu without fzf; outside a terminal an absent reference exits 2 and lists the candidates. Colours appear only on a terminal and follow `NO_COLOR`. A live event is redacted with the credentials and the session references the stream has declared so far; the events bound to the record are derived again from the whole transcript. `on_terminal` on an agent (or else on a purpose) is a command run once after a run ends, with only `POSE_DELEGATE_RUN` and `POSE_DELEGATE_STATE` in its environment, the run directory as working directory, its own process group and a bound (`timeout`, default 30s, at most 5m); its failure is recorded beside the run, shown by `ls`, and never changes the run's state. `pose delegate handoff <run> --to <agent|auto>` continues a run that stopped interrupted or failed, short of a conclusion, with another agent under the same attempt and its continuation budget, which is the `resume.max_resumes` and `max_wall_time` of the agent that opened the attempt (3 and 60 minutes by default) whichever agent continues it, so changing agents never widens it; a completed run, the same agent and a run already continued are refused, and a run handed off is no longer resumed. Its brief is the predecessor's generated brief followed by a labelled section: the predecessor's partial final message and the commands it ran with their exit status, paired by the call identifier the agent's stream records and otherwise left unknown rather than guessed, each with its digest, quoted so no line of it reads as a decision, bounded at 16 KiB with the cut disclosed, and an instruction to re-verify. Only the handoff run concludes. The record binds the base brief digest and the section digest in `handoff_digest`, names its predecessor in `hands_off_from`, and a signed proof verifies the section against the predecessor's record. `--to auto` routes as the purpose does, the stopped agent excluded and unavailable ones skipped. A foreground run or handoff that stops interrupted in a terminal offers waiting for its reset and resuming, handing off, or cancelling, which is offered even alone, and does nothing until answered; the stopping command's result, `ls --json` and `wait --json` carry the same ways forward as `next_actions`, each with its command, whether it is available now — judged by the planning its command runs, budget and wait bound included — and why not, and a `cancel` that leaves the run stopped. `watch` marks a handoff with `⇄` beside the `↻` of a resume. `pose delegate task <prompt-file|->` hands any task to another agent: the prompt goes on stdin in `--cd` (default: the current directory) with the agent's command for `--access read|sandboxed|write|full`; the default is `purposes.task.default_access` (read when unset), `full` is never a default and prints a warning naming what it disables. A task's template places `{add_dirs}`, `{no_network}` and `{outside_git}`, filled from the agent's `add_dir_args` (with `{dir}`), `no_network_args` and `outside_git_args`; `--add-dir`, `--no-network` and `--effort` that the agent's template cannot carry are refused with exit 2, and `--no-network` with `write` or `full` too. The final message goes to stdout and the agent's exit code is returned; `--json` replaces both with one document. A task runs in the real directory: containment bounds its time and output, not its filesystem. Its record (prompt, stream, error stream, normalized events, argv, removed variable names, exit code, label) is owner-only under the user's state directory (`$XDG_STATE_HOME/pose/delegate/`, where `XDG_STATE_HOME` counts only as an absolute path and `~/.local/state` is the default), or, with `--record`, in the `delegate-runs` directory of `.pose`. The state directory may be reached through a link of the user's own; resolved, it and every directory above it must belong to the user or to root and be writable by no group or other user unless sticky, and `pose`, `pose/delegate` and the project's directory below it must be real directories of the user's that no group or other user can write; a task refuses to record or read anywhere else and names the directory (on Windows only links are refused). `--detach` starts a task as the review purposes start a detached run and returns its attempt, its log and the place of its record; a prompt piped to `task -` or `resume --prompt -` is read before detaching and written to the detached run through a pipe, never a file, and a refusal before the attempt is reserved returns the foreground's document and exit code. A detached call keeps its log and hand-back in `.pose/review-private/supervisors`, which, with every directory up to the project root, must be the user's and writable by no group or other user, and whose root's ancestors must belong to the user or root and be writable by no one else unless sticky; the root is resolved once and only that resolved root is used, a caller whose current directory is gone is refused, and a project's private task history is keyed by its resolved root (records an earlier engine kept under a linked spelling are still read). A detached call believes a hand-back only once a piped prompt is through and a run reserved the attempt it names. A checkout that fails this can still run in the foreground; on Windows only links and non-directories are refused, and ACLs are not checked. A task is never evidence: no review reader reads it, `pose review attest --run` refuses it by name, and `wait` returns 0 when the agent succeeded, with no decision. `ls`, `watch`, `wait`, `cancel`, `resume` and `handoff` work on tasks; `resume` without `--prompt` sends a default continuation message, with the agent's `resume.commands` for the task's access, and `--agent auto` routes against the launching session. A task can also continue a session POSE never recorded, such as one a personal launcher opened, with `--session <id> --agent <name>`: the agent's resume command for the access runs with that session, the prompt optional, and the record says it continued a session without keeping it. `pose review dispatch`, `resume` and `status`, the aliases this surface deprecated, were removed: each exits 2 and names its `pose delegate` replacement.\n \n `delegation.json` is schema 2: `agents` (vendor, model, `cli`, `events`, per-access `commands`, `effort`, `timeout`, `limit`, `session`, `resume`, `executor`, `containment`, `on_terminal`, `probe` — `command` and an optional `timeout` up to 60s, default 10s, with no placeholder), `routing` (`prefer`, `on_unavailable`, `lead_models` as `{vendor: model}` in lower case, `max_wait` up to 24h), per-purpose budgets (`purposes.<review|adjudicate|smoke|task>.max_runs_per_bundle`, `timeout`) and `env_unset`. Command templates are argv arrays; `{output}`, `{session}` and `{effort}` are whole elements, never interpolated, and `{effort}` becomes the argv fragment of the agent's effort level. An agent's own `max_runs_per_bundle` wins over the purpose's; a purpose's `timeout` wins over the agent's. `pose update` converts `reviewers.json` (schema 1) into `delegation.json` deterministically and removes it: each adapter becomes an agent whose `read` command is the adapter's command, a budget all adapters share moves to the review-path purposes, and every agent projects exactly the adapter it came from. The converted file is archived outside `.pose/policy` and named in `migrated_from`, so a run recorded before the conversion keeps its policy baseline while `delegation.json` is exactly that conversion; editing `delegation.json` ends that, as any policy change does. The engine reads `reviewers.json` while `delegation.json` is absent and refuses when both exist. A schema 1 adapter that interpolates `{output}` or `{session}` inside an argument (in `sh -c` code, for example) is not converted: `pose update` warns and keeps `reviewers.json` until the template passes the placeholder as a whole element.\ndiff --git a/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md b/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md\nindex 3dae2548..6bb1495e 100644\n--- a/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md\n+++ b/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md\n@@ -375,6 +375,7 @@ pose release-package-manifests --version X.Y.Z --checksums <checksums.txt> --out\n   **Histórico cru:** todo leitor de histórico da revisão estrutural e do grafo de entrega ignora replace refs e grafts; um repositório com algum deles recebe entradas de revisão diferentes das anteriores, então rode `pose index` e sele de novo. Um change set de trailer que começa no primeiro commit do repositório resolve contra a árvore vazia.\n   **Testemunhas de remoção:** um caminho do assunto de revisão removido por uma spec posterior só vale como essa remoção enquanto o Git ainda a mostra: o commit de remoção em `HEAD`, lido cru, sem commit posterior tocando o caminho, em um clone que não seja raso. Do contrário a preparação recusa e pede `pose index`. Uma geração vincula os change sets de `.pose/reports/history` como `recorded-change-sets`, então uma publicada antes aparece como não vinculada até o próximo `pose index`.\n   **Filhos:** um milestone de um único membro local não tem revisão própria e vale pela aprovação fechada dessa spec, e um roadmap pode declarar `unreviewed_milestones` para milestones com todos os membros terminais, selado pela revisão dele. Fora disso, a aprovação de um milestone ou roadmap vale só enquanto a revisão de cada filho vale: um filho rejeitado, desatualizado, com histórico de atestações perdido ou não fechado sob um pai `done` faz o pai deixar de verificar como aprovado, em `review verify`, `review-check` e no closeout, e o pai se recupera com o filho, sem novo selo. A seleção segue a linhagem de substituição: uma substituição do mesmo conteúdo vem antes do que substitui, sejam quais forem as datas de selo, um `replaces` que nomeia outro conteúdo não conta, e a aprovação de um bundle substituído não é retida.\n+  **Política de revisão do fechamento (schema 5):** uma instância que adota `pose adopt closeout-review-policy` decide com `milestone_review_required` e `roadmap_review_required` (`never`, `when-justified`, `always`) se o fechamento de um milestone ou de um roadmap exige revisão própria. `when-justified` exige para milestone com mais de duas specs distintas e para roadmap com mais de uma spec distinta e ao menos uma associação de ADR (`adrs: [adr:<arquivo>.md]` no roadmap, `roadmaps: [roadmap:<slug>]` na ADR, ou um link Markdown local em qualquer direção). `always` exige inclusive para milestone de um membro, e nem a herança do membro único nem `unreviewed_milestones` a enfraquecem. A revisão de cada membro continua exigida; uma revisão registrada no escopo mesmo assim é verificada como antes e sua rejeição vale; membro ou associação que não resolve nunca concede dispensa. Um milestone que fecha sem revisão própria mantém o seu exit gate: o gate vai para o plano de revisão do seu único membro, ou do último (o selado quando todos os outros já são terminais), e o fechamento fica bloqueado até que a revisão aprovada de um membro tenha julgado o gate como ele está, ou até que o milestone seja revisado. Um membro movido por transferência conta no destino, e um milestone ou roadmap local citado em `after:` ou `consumes:` só satisfaz a aresta depois de fechado, não pelo status dos membros. Com a política adotada, `pose close milestone:<roadmap>/<id>` e `pose close roadmap:<slug>` continuam explícitos em todos os modos e publicam um recibo (`review-closeouts/<milestone|roadmap>/…json` dentro de `.pose/`) com `review: required|not-required`, a chave, o valor, `closeout-review@1` e o motivo (`members=1, threshold>2`); o escopo só é terminal com ele. Instalações novas adotam `never`/`never`; uma policy existente mantém a regra legada até adotar, `pose setup` mostra as duas, `pose doctor` lista escopos abertos que devem revisão, e roadmaps fechados antes da adoção são verificados pelo contrato com que foram selados.\n - `close` — aplica uma transição de ciclo de vida com gate de review para concluir uma spec, milestone ou roadmap. A transição só é publicada sobre os bytes que seus gates julgaram, sob a trava de ciclo de vida da spec que start e transfer também tomam (spec `pose-writer-coordination-and-atomic-publication`): uma edição feita enquanto os gates rodavam é um `publish-conflict` que preserva a edição, a autorização é julgada de novo dentro da trava, e um start ou transfer segurando a spec recusa o close com o dono da trava (`start-in-progress` ou `spec-lifecycle-in-progress`). Estado do Git (spec `pose-git-and-dispatch-snapshot-integrity`): um `git status`, HEAD ou árvore que não pôde ser lido é desconhecido, não limpo — o plano de close bloqueia em vez de selar evidência sobre uma árvore que não viu (um diretório sem repositório é outro fato e mantém o comportamento); o snapshot governado, a checagem de path do bundle e o snapshot de obrigações também tratam status ilegível como desconhecido. `review dispatch` compara o worktree de review com a árvore do próprio commit selado antes e depois da execução, de modo que `assume-unchanged`, `skip-worktree`, troca de tipo ou link não escondam uma edição; a regeneração nativa exige outputs exatos do produtor que permaneçam arquivos regulares; o adapter escreve em stdout ou no output externo, e nomes de diretório não autorizam mudanças no worktree de review, e uma execução que termina depois que o bundle foi superado ou a policy mudou é registrada como `stale`: histórico, não o cumprimento vigente do escopo.\n - `continuous-closeout` — persiste e projeta o estado terminal de continuous closeout para workflows de iteração contínua. `status --json` escreve um resultado de operação cujo `data` traz `active` e `terminal`; a saída continua 1 enquanto um escopo ativo não é terminal.\n - **Canais de saída** — o resultado (veredito, findings, dados) vai para o stdout e o progresso (passos, uso, falhas do próprio comando) para o stderr, então `pose x --json | jq` é seguro e `2>/dev/null` nunca esconde um finding. `--json` imprime um documento cujo `severity` do finding é a âncora não traduzida; `--quiet` imprime apenas o veredito; `pose validate --verbose` transmite a saída do check em vez de capturá-la. Cor, símbolos e spinner existem apenas em terminal, resolvidos por stream, e respeitam `--color`, `POSE_COLOR`, `NO_COLOR` e `TERM`; `COLUMNS` define a largura de quebra e `POSE_LOCALE` (`en` ou `pt-BR`) o idioma de toda mensagem que o POSE compõe. Exit codes: 0 aprovado, 1 falha de gate, 2 erro de uso.\n@@ -894,6 +895,10 @@ ou task e seguir próximas ações nativas.\n \n `pose review` governa o sujeito (bundle, brief, atestação, verify); `pose delegate` governa as execuções. Um **agente** é um perfil de CLI configurado em `.pose/policy/delegation.json`; um **run** é uma execução; uma **tentativa** é um run e suas continuações; uma **thread** são as rodadas de um escopo. `pose delegate review`, `adjudicate` e `smoke` rodam o brief gerado do bundle selado num worktree descartável no commit selado, só com acesso de leitura, sob o ledger de tentativas e a contenção abaixo; qualquer outro `--access` é recusado. O agente nunca registra atestação: o motor registra a conclusão de um run de review concluído só como descrevem as conclusões de reviews delegadas abaixo. Sem `--agent` (ou com `auto`) o roteamento escolhe entre os agentes do propósito: primeiro por `routing.prefer` (padrão `opposite-vendor-of-implementation`, na ordem em que a policy declara seus agentes), depois pelos passos de `routing.on_unavailable` (padrão `same-vendor-other-model`, `wait`, `same-model`). `wait` só se aplica quando todo candidato anterior está limitado com reset conhecido dentro de `routing.max_wait` (padrão 5h) e inicia o run depois do primeiro reset, sem polling. A implementação contra a qual se roteia é a identidade que o bundle selou; para um bundle selado antes de os bundles a registrarem, o `provenance.implementation` da policy, senão `--lead`. Um agente nomeado com `--agent` dispensa o roteamento e avisa quando é do vendor da implementação. Todo run registra a decisão (`routing`: os candidatos, por que cada um foi ignorado, o passo tomado) e `independence_achieved` (`different-vendor`, `different-model` ou `same-model`); sem agente disponível o run não começa e o erro traz a decisão. `--label` nomeia o run, `--lead <vendor>[/<modelo>]` declara a implementação quando o bundle não registra nenhuma, `--dry-run` mostra agente, propósito, acesso, diretório de trabalho e argv resolvidos (com os valores de credenciais autorizadas ocultos) e não reserva nada, e `--json` imprime um documento versionado (`pose.delegate.run/v1`, `plan/v1`, `ls/v1`, `cancel/v1`, `agents/v1`, `error/v1`), inclusive erros de uso. Em `ls`, `resume`, `cancel` e `agents` as mesmas flags selecionam: um run ou agente que não corresponde não é listado, ou é recusado quando nomeado; `--dry-run` não altera nada em nenhum comando. As mensagens seguem `POSE_LOCALE` (`en` ou `pt-BR`). Uma referência de run é um id de run, um id de tentativa, um label ou um prefixo de sessão com seis caracteres ou mais; uma referência ambígua sai com 2 e lista os candidatos.\n \n+Um agente schema 2 pode optar por `review_profile.command` e `review_profile.resume_command` somente para review governado. A ausência preserva o adaptador read existente; tasks, adjudicação e smoke mantêm seus comandos. Um agente retomável deve declarar o comando retomado do perfil com `{session}`. Falha do perfil selecionado nunca volta ao perfil read ou de task. Policy e proveniência continuam vinculando os comandos selecionados. Parsers antigos estritos recusam o campo novo. O codec Claude configurado canonicaliza init tipado repetido somente quando identifica a mesma sessão, antes da captura. Identidades diferentes e ambiguidade de regex genérica continuam recusadas; proveniência bruta, redação e canais de limite/reset usam os bytes originais.\n+\n+O perfil de revisão Claude deste repositório usa safe/restricted, somente Read/Grep/Glob/Bash, sandbox obrigatório dos comandos (`failIfUnavailable:true`, `allowUnsandboxedCommands:false`) e source selado sem escrita. Os wrappers fresh e resume exigem bubblewrap, socat e jq no Linux (jq também no macOS), com dica de instalação na recusa. Cache/temp do Go ficam em scratch privado da operação fora do checkout; o cache de módulos existente é lido, e downloads indisponíveis ou testes que escrevem no source podem falhar. O perfil instrui o revisor a rodar `go clean -cache` como último comando Bash dentro do sandbox; o motor confere a limpeza independentemente, com os limites inalterados, e mantém limpeza incompleta como falha. Nunca limpar o cache controlado pelo adaptador fora do sandbox: um teste pode substituir seu caminho por um symlink. A qualificação cobre a fronteira dos subprocessos shell do Claude, não isolamento do executável confiável nem sigilo de credenciais arbitrárias. Checks de integridade do checkout continuam obrigatórios. Instalar o motor não adota o perfil em outra instância; copie e qualifique o bloco explícito da policy antes de usá-lo. O wrapper também nega o Git comum físico resolvido, pois o Claude o permite automaticamente em worktrees. Para esse perfil opt-in, o POSE prepara somente diretórios vazios `.claude/.cc-writes` na raiz descartável e nos componentes declarados antes do snapshot; arquivos novos e alterações posteriores continuam recusados. A limpeza final dentro do sandbox também remove somente diretórios `go-build*` diretamente em GOTMPDIR, depois de todos os jobs terminarem.\n+\n `pose delegate ls` lista as tentativas pelo run mais recente com estado, propósito, agente, label, turnos, idade e último passo — para um run interrompido também a causa, se há draft parcial, o instante de reset e o tempo restante — e os runs em andamento com a vivacidade do processo. `pose delegate cancel <run>` para um run em andamento; ele é registrado `failed` com a causa `cancelled`, e a tentativa continua consumida. `pose delegate agents` lista cada agente com vendor, modelo, se a CLI está no path, o instante de reset do último limite de uso registrado e o estado que o roteamento lê: `available`, `limited until <instante>` (o último run atingiu um limite de uso cujo reset ainda não chegou), `missing` (a CLI não está no path), `unknown` (o histórico de runs não pôde ser lido inteiro) ou `probe-failed` (o `probe` opcional falhou dentro do seu limite). A disponibilidade é lida do path e dos runs registrados; só um probe configurado executa algo. `pose delegate review|adjudicate|smoke --detach` inicia o run como um processo próprio, numa sessão própria que sobrevive ao terminal de quem chamou, e retorna na hora com a tentativa (uma referência de run) e o caminho do seu log; um run cuja rota precisa antes esperar o reset de um limite de uso não é destacado. `pose delegate wait <run> [--timeout <d>] [--json]` é um processo comum em primeiro plano que termina quando o run termina e imprime uma linha — run, estado, decisão, se é retomável, instante de reset — com o código de saída `0` aprovado, `10` mudanças pedidas ou rejeitado, `20` falhou (também um run concluído sem decisão), `30` interrompido, `40` travado (`stalled`), `50` a espera esgotou o tempo, e `2` para erro de uso; todos os que esperam são liberados, um run já encerrado retorna na hora, e matar um wait deixa o run rodando. O processo que roda uma tentativa reescreve um heartbeat no seu marcador a cada 10 segundos; um run cujo heartbeat tem mais de três intervalos e cujo processo comprovadamente não existe mais é registrado `stalled` pelo primeiro leitor, por uma reivindicação exclusiva com que todos os leitores concordam, e `ls` mostra a idade do heartbeat de cada run em andamento. `pose delegate watch [<run>|<label>]` mostra uma tentativa como uma tarefa só: os runs registrados são reproduzidos com um marcador onde um run retomou outro e o run interrompido nomeando o run que o continuou, e uma tentativa em andamento é então acompanhada pelos eventos normalizados e redigidos que o seu processo grava à medida que chegam, até o registro ser publicado; `--no-follow` imprime o que existe e sai, `-v` acrescenta o fim da saída de cada ferramenta (`--lines <n>`, padrão 12) e `--json` imprime NDJSON. Sem referência ele acompanha o único run em andamento; num terminal, caso contrário, abre o fzf com um preview do run sob o cursor atualizado a cada `--refresh <d>` (padrão 2s, 0 desliga), ou um menu numerado sem fzf; fora de um terminal uma referência ausente sai com 2 e lista os candidatos. Cores só aparecem num terminal e seguem `NO_COLOR`. Um evento ao vivo é redigido com as credenciais e as referências de sessão que o stream declarou até ali; os eventos vinculados ao registro são derivados de novo do transcript inteiro. `on_terminal` num agente (ou, senão, num propósito) é um comando executado uma vez depois que um run termina, só com `POSE_DELEGATE_RUN` e `POSE_DELEGATE_STATE` no ambiente, o diretório de runs como diretório de trabalho, um grupo de processos próprio e um limite (`timeout`, padrão 30s, no máximo 5m); a falha dele é registrada ao lado do run, mostrada pelo `ls`, e nunca muda o estado do run. `pose delegate handoff <run> --to <agente|auto>` continua um run que parou interrompido ou falho, sem conclusão, com outro agente na mesma tentativa e no seu orçamento de continuação, que é o `resume.max_resumes` e o `max_wall_time` do agente que abriu a tentativa (3 e 60 minutos por padrão), qualquer que seja o agente que a continua, de modo que trocar de agente nunca o amplia; um run concluído, o mesmo agente e um run já continuado são recusados, e um run repassado não é mais retomado. O brief é o brief gerado do predecessor seguido de uma seção rotulada: a mensagem final parcial do predecessor e os comandos que ele rodou com o status de saída, pareados pelo identificador de chamada que o stream do agente registra e, sem ele, deixados como desconhecidos em vez de adivinhados, cada um com seu digest, citados para que nenhuma linha vire uma decisão, limitada a 16 KiB com o corte declarado, e uma instrução de reverificar. Só o run de handoff conclui. O registro vincula o digest do brief base e o da seção em `handoff_digest`, nomeia o predecessor em `hands_off_from`, e uma prova assinada verifica a seção contra o registro do predecessor. `--to auto` roteia como o propósito, excluindo o agente parado e pulando os indisponíveis. Um run ou handoff em primeiro plano que para interrompido num terminal oferece esperar o reset e retomar, fazer o handoff ou cancelar, oferecido mesmo sozinho, e não faz nada até a resposta; o resultado do comando que parou, `ls --json` e `wait --json` trazem os mesmos caminhos em `next_actions`, cada um com o comando, se está disponível agora — avaliado pelo planejamento que o comando roda, orçamento e limite de espera incluídos — e por que não, e um `cancel` que deixa o run parado. `watch` marca um handoff com `⇄`, ao lado do `↻` de uma retomada. `pose delegate task <prompt-file|->` entrega qualquer tarefa a outro agente: o prompt vai pelo stdin em `--cd` (padrão: o diretório atual) com o comando do agente para `--access read|sandboxed|write|full`; o padrão é `purposes.task.default_access` (read quando ausente), `full` nunca é padrão e imprime um aviso dizendo o que ele desliga. O template de uma task posiciona `{add_dirs}`, `{no_network}` e `{outside_git}`, preenchidos a partir de `add_dir_args` (com `{dir}`), `no_network_args` e `outside_git_args` do agente; `--add-dir`, `--no-network` e `--effort` que o template do agente não comporta são recusados com saída 2, assim como `--no-network` com `write` ou `full`. A mensagem final vai para o stdout e o código de saída do agente é devolvido; `--json` troca os dois por um documento. Uma task roda no diretório real: a contenção limita seu tempo e sua saída, não seu sistema de arquivos. O registro (prompt, stream, stream de erro, eventos normalizados, argv, nomes das variáveis removidas, código de saída, label) fica só para o dono no diretório de estado do usuário (`$XDG_STATE_HOME/pose/delegate/`, em que `XDG_STATE_HOME` só vale como caminho absoluto e o padrão é `~/.local/state`), ou, com `--record`, no diretório `delegate-runs` de `.pose`. O diretório de estado pode ser alcançado por um link do próprio usuário; resolvido, ele e todo diretório acima dele devem pertencer ao usuário ou ao root e não ser graváveis por grupo nem por outros usuários, salvo com sticky, e `pose`, `pose/delegate` e o diretório do projeto abaixo dele devem ser diretórios reais do usuário que nenhum grupo ou outro usuário possa gravar; uma task se recusa a registrar ou ler em qualquer outro lugar e nomeia o diretório (no Windows só links são recusados). `--detach` inicia uma task como os propósitos de review iniciam um run destacado e devolve a tentativa, o log e o lugar do registro; um prompt enviado por pipe a `task -` ou `resume --prompt -` é lido antes de destacar e escrito no run destacado por um pipe, nunca por um arquivo, e uma recusa antes de a tentativa ser reservada devolve o documento e o código de saída do primeiro plano. Uma chamada destacada guarda o log e a devolução em `.pose/review-private/supervisors`, que, com todo diretório até a raiz do projeto, deve ser do usuário e não gravável por grupo nem por outros usuários, e cujos ancestrais da raiz devem pertencer ao usuário ou ao root e não ser graváveis por mais ninguém, salvo com sticky; a raiz é resolvida uma vez e só essa raiz resolvida é usada, quem chama a partir de um diretório que não existe mais é recusado, e o histórico privado de tasks de um projeto é indexado pela raiz resolvida (registros que um motor anterior guardou sob uma grafia com link continuam sendo lidos). Uma chamada destacada só acredita numa devolução depois que o prompt enviado por pipe passou e um run reservou a tentativa que ela nomeia. Um checkout que não atende a isso ainda roda em primeiro plano; no Windows só links e não diretórios são recusados, e ACLs não são verificadas. Uma task nunca é evidência: nenhum leitor de review a lê, `pose review attest --run` a recusa pelo nome, e `wait` devolve 0 quando o agente teve sucesso, sem decisão. `ls`, `watch`, `wait`, `cancel`, `resume` e `handoff` funcionam com tasks; `resume` sem `--prompt` envia uma mensagem padrão de continuação, com os `resume.commands` do agente para o acesso da task, e `--agent auto` roteia contra a sessão que a lançou. Uma task também pode continuar uma sessão que o POSE nunca registrou, como uma que um launcher pessoal abriu, com `--session <id> --agent <nome>`: o comando de resume do agente para o acesso roda com essa sessão, o prompt é opcional, e o registro diz que continuou uma sessão sem guardá-la. `pose review dispatch`, `resume` e `status`, os aliases que esta superfície depreciou, foram removidos: cada um sai com 2 e nomeia o substituto em `pose delegate`.\n \n `delegation.json` é schema 2: `agents` (vendor, modelo, `cli`, `events`, `commands` por acesso, `effort`, `timeout`, `limit`, `session`, `resume`, `executor`, `containment`, `on_terminal`, `probe` — `command` e um `timeout` opcional de até 60s, padrão 10s, sem placeholder), `routing` (`prefer`, `on_unavailable`, `lead_models` como `{vendor: modelo}` em minúsculas, `max_wait` de até 24h), orçamentos por propósito (`purposes.<review|adjudicate|smoke|task>.max_runs_per_bundle`, `timeout`) e `env_unset`. Templates de comando são arrays de argv; `{output}`, `{session}` e `{effort}` são elementos inteiros, nunca interpolados, e `{effort}` vira o fragmento de argv do nível de esforço do agente. O `max_runs_per_bundle` do próprio agente vence o do propósito; o `timeout` do propósito vence o do agente. `pose update` converte `reviewers.json` (schema 1) em `delegation.json` de forma determinística e o remove: cada adapter vira um agente cujo comando `read` é o comando do adapter, um orçamento comum a todos os adapters vai para os propósitos do caminho de review, e cada agente projeta exatamente o adapter de origem. O arquivo convertido é arquivado fora de `.pose/policy` e citado em `migrated_from`, de modo que um run registrado antes da conversão mantém sua baseline de policy enquanto `delegation.json` for exatamente essa conversão; editar `delegation.json` encerra isso, como qualquer mudança de policy. O motor lê `reviewers.json` enquanto `delegation.json` não existe e recusa quando os dois existem. Um adapter schema 1 que interpola `{output}` ou `{session}` dentro de um argumento (em código `sh -c`, por exemplo) não é convertido: `pose update` avisa e mantém `reviewers.json` até o template passar o placeholder como elemento inteiro.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_58","type":"agent_message","text":"The focused closeout-policy tests passed across the domain, CLI, and MCP packages. Code inspection still shows two gaps beyond their assertions: federation reads a transferred member’s original identity, and doctor uses the current policy after finding a receipt nonterminal. I’m checking their exact paths and the remaining receipt and dependency readers.\n"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_59","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc 'nl -ba pose-mcp/internal/pose/closeout_review_policy.go'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_59","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc 'nl -ba pose-mcp/internal/pose/closeout_review_policy.go'","aggregated_output":"     1\tpackage pose\n     2\t\n     3\t// Closeout review policy by cost and benefit (spec\n     4\t// pose-closeout-review-policy-by-cost-benefit, ADR\n     5\t// closeout-review-policy-by-cost-and-benefit). An instance that adopts review\n     6\t// policy schema 5 decides, per aggregate scope kind, whether the close of a\n     7\t// milestone or a roadmap owes its own independent review. Closing stays\n     8\t// explicit in every mode; what changes is only the review of the aggregate.\n     9\t// One evaluator answers for every reader, so plan, verification, state,\n    10\t// doctor and close cannot disagree about the threshold.\n    11\t\n    12\timport (\n    13\t\t\"encoding/json\"\n    14\t\t\"fmt\"\n    15\t\t\"sort\"\n    16\t\t\"strings\"\n    17\t)\n    18\t\n    19\tconst (\n    20\t\tCloseoutReviewNever         = \"never\"\n    21\t\tCloseoutReviewWhenJustified = \"when-justified\"\n    22\t\tCloseoutReviewAlways        = \"always\"\n    23\t\n    24\t\tCloseoutReviewEvaluatorVersion = \"closeout-review@1\"\n    25\t\n    26\t\tMilestoneReviewRequiredKey = \"milestone_review_required\"\n    27\t\tRoadmapReviewRequiredKey   = \"roadmap_review_required\"\n    28\t\n    29\t\t// The when-justified thresholds the maintainer chose: a milestone owes a\n    30\t\t// review with more than two distinct specs; a roadmap with more than one\n    31\t\t// distinct spec and at least one ADR association.\n    32\t\tcloseoutMilestoneMemberThreshold = 2\n    33\t\tcloseoutRoadmapMemberThreshold   = 1\n    34\t)\n    35\t\n    36\tfunc validCloseoutReviewValue(value string) bool {\n    37\t\tswitch value {\n    38\t\tcase CloseoutReviewNever, CloseoutReviewWhenJustified, CloseoutReviewAlways:\n    39\t\t\treturn true\n    40\t\t}\n    41\t\treturn false\n    42\t}\n    43\t\n    44\t// validateCloseoutReviewPolicyKeys refuses the pair outside schema 5, and\n    45\t// under schema 5 refuses a missing, null, mistyped, duplicated or unknown value\n    46\t// before anything plans or writes against it.\n    47\tfunc validateCloseoutReviewPolicyKeys(raw []byte, fields map[string]json.RawMessage, schema int) error {\n    48\t\t_, hasMilestone := fields[MilestoneReviewRequiredKey]\n    49\t\t_, hasRoadmap := fields[RoadmapReviewRequiredKey]\n    50\t\tif schema != CloseoutReviewPolicySchemaVersion {\n    51\t\t\tif hasMilestone || hasRoadmap {\n    52\t\t\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"pose: %s and %s require review policy schema %d\", MilestoneReviewRequiredKey, RoadmapReviewRequiredKey, CloseoutReviewPolicySchemaVersion)\n    53\t\t\t}\n    54\t\t\treturn nil\n    55\t\t}\n    56\t\tif err := rejectDuplicateJSONKeys(raw, false, true); err != nil {\n    57\t\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"pose: invalid schema-%d review policy: %w\", CloseoutReviewPolicySchemaVersion, err)\n    58\t\t}\n    59\t\tif !hasMilestone || !hasRoadmap {\n    60\t\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"pose: review policy schema %d requires both %s and %s\", CloseoutReviewPolicySchemaVersion, MilestoneReviewRequiredKey, RoadmapReviewRequiredKey)\n    61\t\t}\n    62\t\tfor _, key := range []string{MilestoneReviewRequiredKey, RoadmapReviewRequiredKey} {\n    63\t\t\tvar value string\n    64\t\t\tif string(fields[key]) == \"null\" || json.Unmarshal(fields[key], &value) != nil || !validCloseoutReviewValue(value) {\n    65\t\t\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"pose: %s must be one of never, when-justified, always (got %s)\", key, string(fields[key]))\n    66\t\t\t}\n    67\t\t}\n    68\t\treturn nil\n    69\t}\n    70\t\n    71\t// CloseoutReviewAdopted reports a policy that decides aggregate reviews by the\n    72\t// closeout pair. Every other policy keeps the legacy rule unchanged.\n    73\tfunc (p ReviewPolicy) CloseoutReviewAdopted() bool {\n    74\t\treturn p.SchemaVersion == CloseoutReviewPolicySchemaVersion\n    75\t}\n    76\t\n    77\t// CloseoutReviewSetting names the policy key and value that govern a scope kind.\n    78\tfunc (p ReviewPolicy) CloseoutReviewSetting(kind string) (string, string) {\n    79\t\tswitch kind {\n    80\t\tcase \"milestone\":\n    81\t\t\treturn MilestoneReviewRequiredKey, p.MilestoneReviewRequired\n    82\t\tcase \"roadmap\":\n    83\t\t\treturn RoadmapReviewRequiredKey, p.RoadmapReviewRequired\n    84\t\t}\n    85\t\treturn \"\", \"\"\n    86\t}\n    87\t\n    88\t// CloseoutReviewDecision is the evaluator's answer for one aggregate scope.\n    89\t// Projections render it; none of them recomputes a threshold.\n    90\ttype CloseoutReviewDecision struct {\n    91\t\tScope            string `json:\"scope\"`\n    92\t\tAdopted          bool   `json:\"adopted\"`\n    93\t\tEvaluatorVersion string `json:\"evaluator_version,omitempty\"`\n    94\t\tPolicyKey        string `json:\"policy_key,omitempty\"`\n    95\t\tPolicyValue      string `json:\"policy_value,omitempty\"`\n    96\t\t// Required is true when the scope owes its own independent review. An\n    97\t\t// unknown input never yields false.\n    98\t\tRequired bool `json:\"required\"`\n    99\t\t// Known is false when a member or an association could not be resolved:\n   100\t\t// the decision then requires the review and says what is missing.\n   101\t\tKnown       bool     `json:\"known\"`\n   102\t\tReason      string   `json:\"reason,omitempty\"`\n   103\t\tMembers     []string `json:\"members,omitempty\"`\n   104\t\tADRs        []string `json:\"adr_associations,omitempty\"`\n   105\t\tDiagnostics []string `json:\"diagnostics,omitempty\"`\n   106\t}\n   107\t\n   108\t// Review is the literal the closeout receipt records: required or not-required.\n   109\tfunc (d CloseoutReviewDecision) Review() string {\n   110\t\tif d.Required {\n   111\t\t\treturn \"required\"\n   112\t\t}\n   113\t\treturn \"not-required\"\n   114\t}\n   115\t\n   116\t// EvaluateCloseoutReview answers whether an aggregate scope owes its own\n   117\t// review under the adopted policy. A legacy policy returns Adopted=false and\n   118\t// the caller keeps the legacy rule.\n   119\tfunc (s Store) EvaluateCloseoutReview(ref string) (CloseoutReviewDecision, error) {\n   120\t\tpolicy, _, err := s.loadReviewPolicy()\n   121\t\tif err != nil {\n   122\t\t\treturn CloseoutReviewDecision{}, err\n   123\t\t}\n   124\t\treturn s.evaluateCloseoutReview(ref, policy)\n   125\t}\n   126\t\n   127\tfunc (s Store) evaluateCloseoutReview(ref string, policy ReviewPolicy) (CloseoutReviewDecision, error) {\n   128\t\tdecision := CloseoutReviewDecision{Scope: ref, Known: true}\n   129\t\tscope, err := ParseScopeRef(ref)\n   130\t\tif err != nil {\n   131\t\t\treturn decision, err\n   132\t\t}\n   133\t\tif !policy.CloseoutReviewAdopted() || (scope.Kind != \"milestone\" && scope.Kind != \"roadmap\") {\n   134\t\t\treturn decision, nil\n   135\t\t}\n   136\t\tdecision.Adopted = true\n   137\t\tdecision.EvaluatorVersion = CloseoutReviewEvaluatorVersion\n   138\t\tdecision.PolicyKey, decision.PolicyValue = policy.CloseoutReviewSetting(scope.Kind)\n   139\t\tslug := scope.Roadmap\n   140\t\tif scope.Kind == \"roadmap\" {\n   141\t\t\tslug = scope.Slug\n   142\t\t}\n   143\t\trm, err := s.GetRoadmap(slug)\n   144\t\tif err != nil {\n   145\t\t\treturn decision, err\n   146\t\t}\n   147\t\tproject, projectErr := s.WriteProjectID()\n   148\t\tvar raws []string\n   149\t\tif scope.Kind == \"milestone\" {\n   150\t\t\tfound := false\n   151\t\t\tfor _, milestone := range rm.Milestones {\n   152\t\t\t\tif milestone.ID == scope.Milestone {\n   153\t\t\t\t\traws, found = milestone.Specs, true\n   154\t\t\t\t}\n   155\t\t\t}\n   156\t\t\tif !found {\n   157\t\t\t\treturn decision, fmt.Errorf(\"pose: milestone %s not found in roadmap %s\", scope.Milestone, scope.Roadmap)\n   158\t\t\t}\n   159\t\t} else {\n   160\t\t\tfor _, milestone := range rm.Milestones {\n   161\t\t\t\traws = append(raws, milestone.Specs...)\n   162\t\t\t}\n   163\t\t}\n   164\t\tif projectErr != nil {\n   165\t\t\tdecision.Diagnostics = append(decision.Diagnostics, \"the project identity that qualifies local members is unknown: \"+projectErr.Error())\n   166\t\t} else {\n   167\t\t\tdecision.Members, decision.Diagnostics = s.closeoutCanonicalMembers(raws, project)\n   168\t\t}\n   169\t\tif len(decision.Diagnostics) > 0 {\n   170\t\t\tdecision.Known = false\n   171\t\t}\n   172\t\tmembers := len(decision.Members)\n   173\t\tswitch decision.PolicyValue {\n   174\t\tcase CloseoutReviewNever:\n   175\t\t\tdecision.Required = false\n   176\t\t\tdecision.Reason = fmt.Sprintf(\"members=%d, never required\", members)\n   177\t\t\t// never decides without counting, but a member it cannot resolve is\n   178\t\t\t// still a scope it cannot close.\n   179\t\t\treturn decision, nil\n   180\t\tcase CloseoutReviewAlways:\n   181\t\t\tdecision.Required = true\n   182\t\t\tdecision.Reason = fmt.Sprintf(\"members=%d, always required\", members)\n   183\t\t\treturn decision, nil\n   184\t\t}\n   185\t\tif !decision.Known {\n   186\t\t\tdecision.Required = true\n   187\t\t\tdecision.Reason = \"members unknown; an exemption needs every member resolved\"\n   188\t\t\treturn decision, nil\n   189\t\t}\n   190\t\tif scope.Kind == \"milestone\" {\n   191\t\t\tdecision.Required = members > closeoutMilestoneMemberThreshold\n   192\t\t\tdecision.Reason = fmt.Sprintf(\"members=%d, threshold>%d\", members, closeoutMilestoneMemberThreshold)\n   193\t\t\treturn decision, nil\n   194\t\t}\n   195\t\t// The associations are resolved even below the member threshold: an\n   196\t\t// association input that cannot be read never proves an exemption.\n   197\t\tadrs, diagnostics := s.closeoutADRAssociations(rm, project)\n   198\t\tdecision.ADRs = adrs\n   199\t\tif len(diagnostics) > 0 {\n   200\t\t\tdecision.Diagnostics = append(decision.Diagnostics, diagnostics...)\n   201\t\t\tdecision.Known = false\n   202\t\t\tdecision.Required = true\n   203\t\t\tdecision.Reason = fmt.Sprintf(\"members=%d, threshold>%d, adr associations unknown\", members, closeoutRoadmapMemberThreshold)\n   204\t\t\treturn decision, nil\n   205\t\t}\n   206\t\tdecision.Required = members > closeoutRoadmapMemberThreshold && len(adrs) > 0\n   207\t\tdecision.Reason = fmt.Sprintf(\"members=%d, threshold>%d, adrs=%d\", members, closeoutRoadmapMemberThreshold, len(adrs))\n   208\t\treturn decision, nil\n   209\t}\n   210\t\n   211\t// closeoutCanonicalMembers resolves milestone members to their final\n   212\t// identities, xref:<project>/spec:<slug>, sorted and deduplicated. A local\n   213\t// member follows only a verified transfer redirect; an external one is\n   214\t// resolved by the federated resolver or stays unknown. Unknown members are\n   215\t// diagnostics, never a count of zero.\n   216\tfunc (s Store) closeoutCanonicalMembers(raws []string, project string) ([]string, []string) {\n   217\t\tseen := map[string]bool{}\n   218\t\tmembers, diagnostics := []string{}, []string{}\n   219\t\tfor _, raw := range raws {\n   220\t\t\tidentity, diagnostic := s.closeoutCanonicalMember(strings.TrimSpace(raw), project)\n   221\t\t\tif diagnostic != \"\" {\n   222\t\t\t\tdiagnostics = append(diagnostics, diagnostic)\n   223\t\t\t\tcontinue\n   224\t\t\t}\n   225\t\t\tif !seen[identity] {\n   226\t\t\t\tseen[identity] = true\n   227\t\t\t\tmembers = append(members, identity)\n   228\t\t\t}\n   229\t\t}\n   230\t\tsort.Strings(members)\n   231\t\treturn members, diagnostics\n   232\t}\n   233\t\n   234\tfunc (s Store) closeoutCanonicalMember(raw, project string) (string, string) {\n   235\t\tref, err := ParseArtifactRef(raw)\n   236\t\tif err != nil || ref.Kind != \"spec\" || ref.Milestone != \"\" {\n   237\t\t\treturn \"\", fmt.Sprintf(\"member %q is not a spec reference\", raw)\n   238\t\t}\n   239\t\tif ref.Project == \"\" {\n   240\t\t\tref.Project = project\n   241\t\t}\n   242\t\tresolve := func(why string) (string, string) {\n   243\t\t\tif s.FederatedResolver == nil {\n   244\t\t\t\treturn \"\", fmt.Sprintf(\"member %s %s and no federated resolver is bound to follow it\", ref.String(), why)\n   245\t\t\t}\n   246\t\t\tresolved := s.FederatedResolver.Resolve(project, ref.String())\n   247\t\t\tif !resolved.Resolved {\n   248\t\t\t\treturn \"\", fmt.Sprintf(\"member %s did not resolve: %s\", ref.String(), resolved.State)\n   249\t\t\t}\n   250\t\t\tif resolved.CanonicalIdentity != nil {\n   251\t\t\t\treturn resolved.CanonicalIdentity.String(), \"\"\n   252\t\t\t}\n   253\t\t\treturn resolved.Identity.String(), \"\"\n   254\t\t}\n   255\t\tif ref.Project != project {\n   256\t\t\treturn resolve(\"belongs to another project\")\n   257\t\t}\n   258\t\t_, found, err := readSpecTransferRedirect(s.fsys(), s, ref)\n   259\t\tif err != nil {\n   260\t\t\treturn \"\", fmt.Sprintf(\"member %s has an unverifiable redirect: %v\", ref.String(), err)\n   261\t\t}\n   262\t\tif found {\n   263\t\t\t// The resolver follows the whole chain, with its cycle and depth\n   264\t\t\t// limits and the destination's authorization: the immediate\n   265\t\t\t// destination may itself have moved.\n   266\t\t\treturn resolve(\"was transferred\")\n   267\t\t}\n   268\t\tif _, err := s.GetSpec(ref.Slug); err != nil {\n   269\t\t\treturn \"\", fmt.Sprintf(\"member %s names no spec of this project\", ref.String())\n   270\t\t}\n   271\t\treturn ref.String(), \"\"\n   272\t}\n   273\t\n   274\t// CloseoutMemberTarget resolves a milestone member to the spec whose closeout\n   275\t// stands for it, as the closeout review policy counts it: a local member a\n   276\t// verified transfer moved is followed to its canonical destination, which is\n   277\t// local (its slug) or external (its identity, judged by federated\n   278\t// acceptance). A redirect that cannot be followed is reported, never read as\n   279\t// the redirect stub the transfer left behind (review round 6, N24).\n   280\tfunc (s Store) CloseoutMemberTarget(raw string) (string, bool, string) {\n   281\t\tslug, external := s.milestoneMember(raw)\n   282\t\tif external {\n   283\t\t\treturn slug, true, \"\"\n   284\t\t}\n   285\t\t// The project the store is bound to, as milestoneMember reads a\n   286\t\t// qualified member, else the one the root declares.\n   287\t\tproject := s.FederatedProjectID\n   288\t\tif project == \"\" {\n   289\t\t\tid, err := s.WriteProjectID()\n   290\t\t\tif err != nil {\n   291\t\t\t\treturn \"\", false, \"member \" + raw + \" cannot be resolved: \" + err.Error()\n   292\t\t\t}\n   293\t\t\tproject = id\n   294\t\t}\n   295\t\t_, found, err := readSpecTransferRedirect(s.fsys(), s, ArtifactRef{Project: project, Kind: \"spec\", Slug: slug})\n   296\t\tif err != nil {\n   297\t\t\treturn \"\", false, \"member \" + raw + \" has an unverifiable redirect: \" + err.Error()\n   298\t\t}\n   299\t\tif !found {\n   300\t\t\treturn slug, false, \"\"\n   301\t\t}\n   302\t\tif s.FederatedResolver == nil || s.FederatedResolver.Roots == nil {\n   303\t\t\tresolver, _, err := EnvironmentArtifactResolverIn(s.fsys(), s.Root, \"\")\n   304\t\t\tif err != nil {\n   305\t\t\t\treturn \"\", false, \"member \" + raw + \" was transferred and no resolver can follow it: \" + err.Error()\n   306\t\t\t}\n   307\t\t\ts.FederatedResolver = &resolver\n   308\t\t}\n   309\t\tcanonical, diagnostic := s.closeoutCanonicalMember(strings.TrimSpace(raw), project)\n   310\t\tif diagnostic != \"\" {\n   311\t\t\treturn \"\", false, diagnostic\n   312\t\t}\n   313\t\tref, err := ParseArtifactRef(canonical)\n   314\t\tif err != nil {\n   315\t\t\treturn \"\", false, \"member \" + raw + \" resolved to \" + canonical + \", which is not a spec reference\"\n   316\t\t}\n   317\t\tif ref.Project == \"\" || ref.Project == project {\n   318\t\t\treturn ref.Slug, false, \"\"\n   319\t\t}\n   320\t\treturn canonical, true, \"\"\n   321\t}\n   322\t\n   323\t// localScopeClosed reports a local milestone or roadmap a dependency edge\n   324\t// names as closed by the terminal projection, under an adopted closeout\n   325\t// review policy where status alone never closes it. A scope already asking\n   326\t// along the chain is a cycle and is not proven closed.\n   327\tfunc (s Store) localScopeClosed(scope string) bool {\n   328\t\tfor _, asking := range s.closeoutChain {\n   329\t\t\tif asking == scope {\n   330\t\t\t\treturn false\n   331\t\t\t}\n   332\t\t}\n   333\t\ts.closeoutChain = append(append([]string{}, s.closeoutChain...), scope)\n   334\t\tclosed, err := s.closedByReceipt(scope)\n   335\t\treturn err == nil && closed\n   336\t}\n   337\t\n   338\t// CurrentCloseoutDecision is the decision for an open aggregate scope now:\n   339\t// the evaluator, then every protection that can only make it stronger. It is\n   340\t// what close judges before publishing and again inside the protected region.\n   341\tfunc (s Store) CurrentCloseoutDecision(ref string) (CloseoutReviewDecision, error) {\n   342\t\tpolicy, _, err := s.loadReviewPolicy()\n   343\t\tif err != nil {\n   344\t\t\treturn CloseoutReviewDecision{}, err\n   345\t\t}\n   346\t\treturn s.currentCloseoutDecision(ref, policy)\n   347\t}\n   348\t\n   349\tfunc (s Store) currentCloseoutDecision(ref string, policy ReviewPolicy) (CloseoutReviewDecision, error) {\n   350\t\tdecision, err := s.evaluateCloseoutReview(ref, policy)\n   351\t\tif err != nil || !decision.Adopted || decision.Required {\n   352\t\t\treturn decision, err\n   353\t\t}\n   354\t\tscope, _ := ParseScopeRef(ref)\n   355\t\ts.protectCloseoutDecision(scope, &decision)\n   356\t\tif !decision.Required && s.mandatoryHumanReview(ref, scope.Kind, policy) {\n   357\t\t\tdecision.Required = true\n   358\t\t\tdecision.Reason += \"; a mandatory-human review is never dispensed\"\n   359\t\t}\n   360\t\treturn decision, nil\n   361\t}\n   362\t\n   363\t// mandatoryHumanReview reports a scope whose effective review plan asks for a\n   364\t// human reviewer: the base profile, the overlays the plan selects for it with\n   365\t// their cutoffs, and the protected baseline when the scope changes the review\n   366\t// contract, exactly as the plan composes them. No setting dispenses that\n   367\t// review. A plan that cannot be built is treated as asking for one, unless the\n   368\t// policy names no profile for the kind, where no review can be asked at all.\n   369\tfunc (s Store) mandatoryHumanReview(ref, kind string, policy ReviewPolicy) bool {\n   370\t\tif policy.ReviewerIndependence[kind] == \"mandatory-human\" {\n   371\t\t\treturn true\n   372\t\t}\n   373\t\tif policy.Profiles[kind] == \"\" {\n   374\t\t\t// No profile, no review to ask for, unless the scope itself removed\n   375\t\t\t// the profile: then the contract it changes cannot exempt it.\n   376\t\t\tscope, err := ParseScopeRef(ref)\n   377\t\t\treturn err != nil || len(s.resolveReviewGovernanceGuard(scope).Paths) > 0\n   378\t\t}\n   379\t\tplanner := s\n   380\t\tplanner.planWithoutCloseoutGate = true\n   381\t\tplan, err := planner.ReviewPlan(ref)\n   382\t\tif err != nil || plan.Independence == \"mandatory-human\" {\n   383\t\t\treturn true\n   384\t\t}\n   385\t\t// The plan composes the overlays' independence; the base profile's own is\n   386\t\t// read from the profile the plan named.\n   387\t\tbase, _, err := s.loadReviewProfile(plan.BaseProfile)\n   388\t\treturn err != nil || base.Independence == \"mandatory-human\"\n   389\t}\n   390\t\n   391\t// milestoneExitGateCriterionID names the criterion that carries a\n   392\t// milestone's exit gate into a member's review.\n   393\tfunc milestoneExitGateCriterionID(roadmap, milestone string) string {\n   394\t\treturn \"milestone-exit-gate-\" + roadmap + \"-\" + milestone\n   395\t}\n   396\t\n   397\tfunc milestoneExitGateCriterionDescription(roadmap, milestone, gate string) string {\n   398\t\treturn \"The exit gate of milestone:\" + roadmap + \"/\" + milestone + \" holds: \" + gate\n   399\t}\n   400\t\n   401\t// lastOpenMemberOf reports a spec that, under an adopted closeout review\n   402\t// policy, carries its multi-member milestone's exit gate into its own review:\n   403\t// it is a local member and every other member is terminal, so its review is\n   404\t// the last one of the milestone. A member of another project is never known\n   405\t// terminal here, so the gate is not carried and the milestone needs its own\n   406\t// review to judge it.\n   407\tfunc (s Store) lastOpenMemberOf(roadmap string, milestone Milestone, slug string) bool {\n   408\t\tif len(milestone.Specs) < 2 {\n   409\t\t\treturn false\n   410\t\t}\n   411\t\tmember := false\n   412\t\tfor _, raw := range milestone.Specs {\n   413\t\t\tother, external := s.milestoneMember(raw)\n   414\t\t\tif external {\n   415\t\t\t\treturn false\n   416\t\t\t}\n   417\t\t\tother = strings.TrimPrefix(other, \"spec:\")\n   418\t\t\tif other == slug {\n   419\t\t\t\tmember = true\n   420\t\t\t\tcontinue\n   421\t\t\t}\n   422\t\t\tsp, err := s.GetSpec(other)\n   423\t\t\tif err != nil || !terminalStatuses[sp.Status] {\n   424\t\t\t\treturn false\n   425\t\t\t}\n   426\t\t}\n   427\t\treturn member\n   428\t}\n   429\t\n   430\t// exitGateJudgedByMember reports a multi-member milestone whose exit gate an\n   431\t// approved member review judged: the member's governing bundle carried the\n   432\t// gate's criterion with the gate as it reads now. A gate edited after that\n   433\t// review is judged by nobody yet.\n   434\tfunc (s Store) exitGateJudgedByMember(roadmap string, milestone Milestone, children []CloseoutState) bool {\n   435\t\tid := milestoneExitGateCriterionID(roadmap, milestone.ID)\n   436\t\tdescription := milestoneExitGateCriterionDescription(roadmap, milestone.ID, milestone.ExitGate)\n   437\t\tfor _, child := range children {\n   438\t\t\tif !child.Review.Approved || child.Review.BundleID == \"\" {\n   439\t\t\t\tcontinue\n   440\t\t\t}\n   441\t\t\tbundle, err := s.LoadReviewBundle(child.Review.BundleID)\n   442\t\t\tif err != nil {\n   443\t\t\t\tcontinue\n   444\t\t\t}\n   445\t\t\tfor _, criterion := range bundle.Payload.Plan.Criteria {\n   446\t\t\t\tif criterion.ID == id && criterion.Description == description && criterion.Required {\n   447\t\t\t\t\treturn true\n   448\t\t\t\t}\n   449\t\t\t}\n   450\t\t}\n   451\t\treturn false\n   452\t}\n   453\t\n   454\tfunc (s Store) milestoneExitGate(scope ScopeRef) string {\n   455\t\trm, err := s.GetRoadmap(scope.Roadmap)\n   456\t\tif err != nil {\n   457\t\t\treturn \"unknown\"\n   458\t\t}\n   459\t\tfor _, milestone := range rm.Milestones {\n   460\t\t\tif milestone.ID == scope.Milestone {\n   461\t\t\t\treturn strings.TrimSpace(milestone.ExitGate)\n   462\t\t\t}\n   463\t\t}\n   464\t\treturn \"unknown\"\n   465\t}\n   466\t\n   467\t// closeoutReviewGate is the one place the readers of an approval ask whether\n   468\t// an aggregate scope owes its own review under an adopted policy. It returns\n   469\t// the decision that governs the scope and whether its own review is dispensed.\n   470\t//\n   471\t// Order (spec pose-closeout-review-policy-by-cost-benefit, \"One evaluator and\n   472\t// precedence\"): a receipt already published decides by what it recorded, as\n   473\t// long as the scope it closed is still the scope; a roadmap closed before\n   474\t// adoption keeps its sealed contract (nil decision); an open scope is decided\n   475\t// now. A review recorded on the scope, required or not, is never bypassed: it\n   476\t// is verified as before and its veto stands. An unknown input never exempts.\n   477\tfunc (s Store) closeoutReviewGate(ref string, policy ReviewPolicy) (*CloseoutReviewDecision, bool, error) {\n   478\t\tif !policy.CloseoutReviewAdopted() {\n   479\t\t\treturn nil, false, nil\n   480\t\t}\n   481\t\tscope, err := ParseScopeRef(ref)\n   482\t\tif err != nil || (scope.Kind != \"milestone\" && scope.Kind != \"roadmap\") {\n   483\t\t\treturn nil, false, err\n   484\t\t}\n   485\t\treceipt, err := s.ReadCloseoutReceipt(ref)\n   486\t\tif err != nil {\n   487\t\t\treturn nil, false, err\n   488\t\t}\n   489\t\tif receipt != nil {\n   490\t\t\tif err := s.closeoutReceiptStands(*receipt, policy); err != nil {\n   491\t\t\t\treturn nil, false, err\n   492\t\t\t}\n   493\t\t\tdecision := receipt.Decision\n   494\t\t\treturn &decision, !decision.Required && !s.scopeReviewRecorded(ref), nil\n   495\t\t}\n   496\t\tif s.closeoutScopeHistorical(scope) || s.milestoneClosedBeforeAdoption(scope) {\n   497\t\t\treturn nil, false, nil\n   498\t\t}\n   499\t\tdecision, err := s.currentCloseoutDecision(ref, policy)\n   500\t\tif err != nil {\n   501\t\t\treturn nil, false, err\n   502\t\t}\n   503\t\treturn &decision, !decision.Required && decision.Known && !s.scopeReviewRecorded(ref), nil\n   504\t}\n   505\t\n   506\t// closeoutReceiptStands checks a receipt against the scope it closed: the\n   507\t// same canonical members. A receipt is never re-decided under a later policy,\n   508\t// but it does not carry over to a scope whose composition changed.\n   509\tfunc (s Store) closeoutReceiptStands(receipt CloseoutReceipt, policy ReviewPolicy) error {\n   510\t\t// The closed contract, read without the current policy: a later policy\n   511\t\t// never re-decides a receipt, but a scope whose composition or exit gate\n   512\t\t// changed is no longer the scope it closed.\n   513\t\tmembers, gate, diagnostics, err := s.closeoutSubject(receipt.Scope)\n   514\t\tif err != nil {\n   515\t\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"pose: closeout receipt of %s cannot be checked against its scope: %w\", receipt.Scope, err)\n   516\t\t}\n   517\t\tif len(diagnostics) > 0 {\n   518\t\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"pose: closeout receipt of %s cannot be checked against its scope: %s\", receipt.Scope, strings.Join(diagnostics, \"; \"))\n   519\t\t}\n   520\t\tdrift := func(what string) error {\n   521\t\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"pose: closeout receipt of %s no longer matches its scope: its %s changed since the close; a receipt is never rewritten, so restore it or record the change in a new scope\", receipt.Scope, what)\n   522\t\t}\n   523\t\tif strings.Join(members, \",\") != strings.Join(receipt.Decision.Members, \",\") {\n   524\t\t\treturn drift(fmt.Sprintf(\"members (closed with %v, now %v)\", receipt.Decision.Members, members))\n   525\t\t}\n   526\t\tif receipt.ExitGateDigest != gate {\n   527\t\t\treturn drift(\"exit gate\")\n   528\t\t}\n   529\t\treturn nil\n   530\t}\n   531\t\n   532\t// protectCloseoutDecision restores the review when the scope itself changes\n   533\t// the review contract: an exemption the diff introduced cannot exempt the diff.\n   534\t// The decision is made a second time under the policy at the change set's base\n   535\t// revision, and a stronger answer there wins. Protection that cannot be\n   536\t// established keeps the review required.\n   537\tfunc (s Store) protectCloseoutDecision(scope ScopeRef, decision *CloseoutReviewDecision) {\n   538\t\tguard := s.resolveReviewGovernanceGuard(scope)\n   539\t\tif len(guard.Paths) == 0 {\n   540\t\t\treturn\n   541\t\t}\n   542\t\trestore := func(reason string) {\n   543\t\t\tdecision.Required = true\n   544\t\t\tdecision.Reason += \"; \" + reason\n   545\t\t}\n   546\t\tif guard.Revision == \"\" {\n   547\t\t\trestore(\"the scope changes the review contract and no protected baseline resolves: \" + guard.Reason)\n   548\t\t\treturn\n   549\t\t}\n   550\t\tbase, err := s.parseReviewPolicyAt(guard.Revision)\n   551\t\tif err != nil {\n   552\t\t\trestore(\"the protected baseline policy cannot be read: \" + err.Error())\n   553\t\t\treturn\n   554\t\t}\n   555\t\tif !base.Enabled && !base.CloseoutReviewAdopted() {\n   556\t\t\treturn\n   557\t\t}\n   558\t\tif base.ReviewerIndependence[scope.Kind] == \"mandatory-human\" {\n   559\t\t\trestore(\"the protected baseline at \" + guard.Revision + \" requires a mandatory-human review\")\n   560\t\t\treturn\n   561\t\t}\n   562\t\t// The baseline's base profile and every overlay it adopted for this kind,\n   563\t\t// selected or not: protection is the conservative reading.\n   564\t\trefs := append([]string{}, base.OverlayProfiles...)\n   565\t\tif ref := base.Profiles[scope.Kind]; ref != \"\" {\n   566\t\t\trefs = append(refs, ref)\n   567\t\t}\n   568\t\tfor _, ref := range refs {\n   569\t\t\tprofile, err := s.parseReviewProfileAt(guard.Revision, ref)\n   570\t\t\tif err != nil || (profile.Scope == scope.Kind || ref == base.Profiles[scope.Kind]) && profile.Independence == \"mandatory-human\" {\n   571\t\t\t\trestore(\"the protected baseline at \" + guard.Revision + \" requires a mandatory-human review or a profile of it cannot be read\")\n   572\t\t\t\treturn\n   573\t\t\t}\n   574\t\t}\n   575\t\tif !base.CloseoutReviewAdopted() {\n   576\t\t\trestore(\"the protected baseline at \" + guard.Revision + \" keeps the legacy rule, under which this scope owes its review\")\n   577\t\t\treturn\n   578\t\t}\n   579\t\tprotected, err := s.evaluateCloseoutReview(decision.Scope, base)\n   580\t\tif err != nil || protected.Required {\n   581\t\t\trestore(\"the protected baseline at \" + guard.Revision + \" requires the review (\" + protected.PolicyKey + \"=\" + protected.PolicyValue + \")\")\n   582\t\t}\n   583\t}\n   584\t\n   585\t// closeoutSubject is what a closeout receipt binds: the canonical members of\n   586\t// the scope and, for a milestone, the digest of its exit gate (a roadmap's is\n   587\t// the gates of its milestones, which their own receipts bind).\n   588\tfunc (s Store) closeoutSubject(ref string) ([]string, string, []string, error) {\n   589\t\tscope, err := ParseScopeRef(ref)\n   590\t\tif err != nil {\n   591\t\t\treturn nil, \"\", nil, err\n   592\t\t}\n   593\t\tslug := scope.Roadmap\n   594\t\tif scope.Kind == \"roadmap\" {\n   595\t\t\tslug = scope.Slug\n   596\t\t}\n   597\t\trm, err := s.GetRoadmap(slug)\n   598\t\tif err != nil {\n   599\t\t\treturn nil, \"\", nil, err\n   600\t\t}\n   601\t\tproject, err := s.WriteProjectID()\n   602\t\tif err != nil {\n   603\t\t\treturn nil, \"\", []string{\"the project identity is unknown: \" + err.Error()}, nil\n   604\t\t}\n   605\t\tvar raws []string\n   606\t\tgate := \"\"\n   607\t\tfor _, milestone := range rm.Milestones {\n   608\t\t\tif scope.Kind == \"milestone\" && milestone.ID != scope.Milestone {\n   609\t\t\t\tcontinue\n   610\t\t\t}\n   611\t\t\traws = append(raws, milestone.Specs...)\n   612\t\t\tif scope.Kind == \"milestone\" {\n   613\t\t\t\tgate = digestText(milestone.ExitGate)\n   614\t\t\t}\n   615\t\t}\n   616\t\tif scope.Kind == \"roadmap\" {\n   617\t\t\t// A roadmap's gate is its cut criteria; its milestones' gates are\n   618\t\t\t// bound by their own receipts.\n   619\t\t\tcriteria, problems := ParseRoadmapCriteria(*rm)\n   620\t\t\traw, _ := json.Marshal(struct {\n   621\t\t\t\tCriteria []RoadmapCriterion\n   622\t\t\t\tProblems []string\n   623\t\t\t}{criteria, problems})\n   624\t\t\tgate = \"sha256:\" + digestHex(raw)\n   625\t\t}\n   626\t\tmembers, diagnostics := s.closeoutCanonicalMembers(raws, project)\n   627\t\treturn members, gate, diagnostics, nil\n   628\t}\n   629\t\n   630\t// closeoutScopeHistorical reports a scope whose roadmap was closed before\n   631\t// adoption: done, with no receipt and no close of this engine in progress,\n   632\t// and with positive evidence of its legacy closure: an approval recorded on\n   633\t// the roadmap, or the legacy rule exempting it as done before review\n   634\t// adoption. Status alone proves nothing.\n   635\t// Its milestones and itself are verified by the contract they were sealed\n   636\t// under, never re-evaluated, and no receipt is backfilled for them. A close\n   637\t// interrupted between its status and its receipt left an intent, so it stays\n   638\t// incomplete instead of reading as history.\n   639\tfunc (s Store) closeoutScopeHistorical(scope ScopeRef) bool {\n   640\t\tslug := scope.Slug\n   641\t\tif scope.Kind == \"milestone\" {\n   642\t\t\tslug = scope.Roadmap\n   643\t\t}\n   644\t\trm, err := s.GetRoadmap(slug)\n   645\t\tif err != nil || rm.Status != \"done\" {\n   646\t\t\treturn false\n   647\t\t}\n   648\t\tref := \"roadmap:\" + slug\n   649\t\tif receipt, err := s.ReadCloseoutReceipt(ref); err != nil || receipt != nil {\n   650\t\t\treturn false\n   651\t\t}\n   652\t\tpending, err := s.closeoutIntentPending(ref)\n   653\t\tif err != nil || pending {\n   654\t\t\treturn false\n   655\t\t}\n   656\t\tpolicy, _, err := s.loadReviewPolicy()\n   657\t\tif err != nil {\n   658\t\t\treturn false\n   659\t\t}\n   660\t\tif required, err := s.reviewRequiredForScope(ScopeRef{Kind: \"roadmap\", Slug: slug}, policy); err == nil && !required {\n   661\t\t\treturn true\n   662\t\t}\n   663\t\trecorded, refused, err := s.milestonesWithRecordedApproval([]string{ref})\n   664\t\treturn err == nil && recorded[ref] && !refused[ref]\n   665\t}\n   666\t\n   667\t// milestoneClosedBeforeAdoption reports a milestone with positive terminal\n   668\t// evidence from before adoption: its own approved review, sealed under a\n   669\t// policy that had not adopted the closeout pair. A milestone merely reached\n   670\t// has no such record and owes its close.\n   671\tfunc (s Store) milestoneClosedBeforeAdoption(scope ScopeRef) bool {\n   672\t\tif scope.Kind != \"milestone\" {\n   673\t\t\treturn false\n   674\t\t}\n   675\t\tref := scope.String()\n   676\t\trecorded, refused, err := s.milestonesWithRecordedApproval([]string{ref})\n   677\t\tif err != nil || !recorded[ref] || refused[ref] {\n   678\t\t\treturn false\n   679\t\t}\n   680\t\tlatest, err := s.latestBundlesOf([]string{ref})\n   681\t\tif err != nil || len(latest[ref]) == 0 {\n   682\t\t\treturn false\n   683\t\t}\n   684\t\tfor _, id := range latest[ref] {\n   685\t\t\tbundle, err := s.LoadReviewBundle(id)\n   686\t\t\tif err != nil || bundle.Payload.Plan.CloseoutReview != nil {\n   687\t\t\t\treturn false\n   688\t\t\t}\n   689\t\t}\n   690\t\treturn true\n   691\t}\n   692\t\n   693\t// closedByReceipt reports an aggregate scope that reads closed: a receipt\n   694\t// that still stands for the scope as it is, or, with no receipt, a verified\n   695\t// closure from before adoption (a historical roadmap's scopes, or a milestone\n   696\t// approved under a plan without a closeout decision), never backfilled with a\n   697\t// receipt. Either way the scope must pass the whole terminal verifier closeout\n   698\t// applies: its review, a refusal recorded later, its children, its gates, and\n   699\t// the acceptance of its dependency edges (predecessors, consumed outcomes,\n   700\t// members), local and remote. It is the one terminal projection reached,\n   701\t// roadmap-reached, attention and doctor ask.\n   702\tfunc (s Store) closedByReceipt(ref string) (bool, error) {\n   703\t\tif len(s.closeoutChain) == 0 || s.closeoutChain[len(s.closeoutChain)-1] != ref {\n   704\t\t\ts.closeoutChain = append(append([]string{}, s.closeoutChain...), ref)\n   705\t\t}\n   706\t\treceipt, err := s.ReadCloseoutReceipt(ref)\n   707\t\tif err != nil {\n   708\t\t\treturn false, err\n   709\t\t}\n   710\t\tif receipt != nil {\n   711\t\t\tpolicy, _, err := s.loadReviewPolicy()\n   712\t\t\tif err != nil {\n   713\t\t\t\treturn false, err\n   714\t\t\t}\n   715\t\t\tif s.closeoutReceiptStands(*receipt, policy) != nil {\n   716\t\t\t\treturn false, nil\n   717\t\t\t}\n   718\t\t} else {\n   719\t\t\tscope, err := ParseScopeRef(ref)\n   720\t\t\tif err != nil {\n   721\t\t\t\treturn false, err\n   722\t\t\t}\n   723\t\t\tif !s.closeoutScopeHistorical(scope) && !s.milestoneClosedBeforeAdoption(scope) {\n   724\t\t\t\treturn false, nil\n   725\t\t\t}\n   726\t\t}\n   727\t\treturn s.closeoutTerminalWithDependencies(ref), nil\n   728\t}\n   729\t\n   730\t// closeoutTerminalWithDependencies runs the terminal verifier with dependency\n   731\t// acceptance, through the store's bound resolver or, without one, the\n   732\t// resolver the environment binds to this root, as `pose close` does. A\n   733\t// resolver that cannot be built leaves the edges unjudged, so the scope does\n   734\t// not read closed. Same-project edges are judged by their lifecycle and\n   735\t// revision, without recursion into their closeout, and remote recursion stops\n   736\t// at a cycle, so the projection converges.\n   737\tfunc (s Store) closeoutTerminalWithDependencies(ref string) bool {\n   738\t\tif s.FederatedResolver == nil || s.FederatedResolver.Roots == nil {\n   739\t\t\tresolver, project, err := EnvironmentArtifactResolverIn(s.fsys(), s.Root, \"\")\n   740\t\t\tif err != nil {\n   741\t\t\t\treturn false\n   742\t\t\t}\n   743\t\t\ts.FederatedProjectID, s.FederatedResolver = project, &resolver\n   744\t\t}\n   745\t\tstate, err := s.getCloseoutState(ref, true)\n   746\t\treturn err == nil && state.Terminal\n   747\t}\n   748\t\n   749\t// scopeReviewRecorded reports a review of the scope on file: a bundle or a\n   750\t// legacy attempt. A record that cannot be read counts as recorded, so an\n   751\t// unreadable rejection never turns into an exemption.\n   752\tfunc (s Store) scopeReviewRecorded(ref string) bool {\n   753\t\tbundles, err := s.ReadReviewBundles(ref)\n   754\t\tif err != nil || len(bundles.Bundles) > 0 || !bundles.Coverage.CompleteFor(ref) {\n   755\t\t\treturn true\n   756\t\t}\n   757\t\tattempts, err := s.ReadReviewAttempts(ref)\n   758\t\treturn err != nil || len(attempts.Attempts) > 0 || !attempts.Coverage.CompleteFor(ref)\n   759\t}\n   760\t\n   761\t// closeoutApprovalOrigin is the origin an exempt scope's approval reports.\n   762\tfunc closeoutApprovalOrigin(decision CloseoutReviewDecision) string {\n   763\t\treturn \"not-required:\" + decision.PolicyKey + \"=\" + decision.PolicyValue\n   764\t}\n   765\t\n   766\tfunc closeoutExemptionWarning(ref string, decision CloseoutReviewDecision) string {\n   767\t\treturn \"review: not-required for \" + ref + \" (\" + decision.PolicyKey + \"=\" + decision.PolicyValue + \", \" + decision.Reason + \"); `pose close \" + ref + \"` is still required\"\n   768\t}\n   769\t\n   770\t// CloseoutReviewOwing is an open aggregate scope that owes its own review under\n   771\t// the adopted policy and holds no recorded approval yet, for `pose doctor`.\n   772\ttype CloseoutReviewOwing struct {\n   773\t\tScope    string                 `json:\"scope\"`\n   774\t\tDecision CloseoutReviewDecision `json:\"decision\"`\n   775\t\t// Previous is the rule that applied before, when a recorded input shows\n   776\t\t// it; with no snapshot it says the comparison is unknown.\n   777\t\tPrevious string `json:\"previous\"`\n   778\t\tAction   string `json:\"action\"`\n   779\t}\n   780\t\n   781\t// CloseoutReviewsOwed lists the open milestones and roadmaps that, under the\n   782\t// adopted closeout review policy, owe a review they do not have. A legacy\n   783\t// policy returns nothing. Close remains the authoritative gate.\n   784\tfunc (s Store) CloseoutReviewsOwed() ([]CloseoutReviewOwing, error) {\n   785\t\tpolicy, _, err := s.loadReviewPolicy()\n   786\t\tif err != nil || !policy.CloseoutReviewAdopted() {\n   787\t\t\treturn nil, err\n   788\t\t}\n   789\t\troadmaps, err := s.ListRoadmaps()\n   790\t\tif err != nil {\n   791\t\t\treturn nil, err\n   792\t\t}\n   793\t\trefs := []string{}\n   794\t\tfor _, rm := range roadmaps {\n   795\t\t\tfor _, milestone := range rm.Milestones {\n   796\t\t\t\trefs = append(refs, \"milestone:\"+rm.Slug+\"/\"+milestone.ID)\n   797\t\t\t}\n   798\t\t\trefs = append(refs, \"roadmap:\"+rm.Slug)\n   799\t\t}\n   800\t\towing := []CloseoutReviewOwing{}\n   801\t\tfor _, ref := range refs {\n   802\t\t\tclosed, err := s.closedByReceipt(ref)\n   803\t\t\tif err != nil {\n   804\t\t\t\treturn nil, err\n   805\t\t\t}\n   806\t\t\tif closed {\n   807\t\t\t\tcontinue\n   808\t\t\t}\n   809\t\t\t// A scope closed before adoption is never re-decided under the\n   810\t\t\t// current policy, but its terminal evidence must still stand: one\n   811\t\t\t// that no longer verifies is listed with the check that says why\n   812\t\t\t// (review round 6, N23).\n   813\t\t\tif scope, err := ParseScopeRef(ref); err == nil && (s.closeoutScopeHistorical(scope) || s.milestoneClosedBeforeAdoption(scope)) {\n   814\t\t\t\towing = append(owing, CloseoutReviewOwing{\n   815\t\t\t\t\tScope:    ref,\n   816\t\t\t\t\tDecision: CloseoutReviewDecision{Scope: ref, Adopted: true, Known: true, Required: true, Reason: \"closed before adoption; its sealed closure no longer verifies\"},\n   817\t\t\t\t\tPrevious: \"closed before adoption under the legacy rule\",\n   818\t\t\t\t\tAction:   \"run `pose closeout-check \" + ref + \"` and remediate the review or child it names; the closure is verified by the contract it was sealed under\",\n   819\t\t\t\t})\n   820\t\t\t\tcontinue\n   821\t\t\t}\n   822\t\t\tdecision, err := s.currentCloseoutDecision(ref, policy)\n   823\t\t\tif err != nil {\n   824\t\t\t\treturn nil, err\n   825\t\t\t}\n   826\t\t\tif !decision.Required {\n   827\t\t\t\tcontinue\n   828\t\t\t}\n   829\t\t\t// A recorded approval counts only while it satisfies the current\n   830\t\t\t// obligation: fresh, for the scope as it is now.\n   831\t\t\tif eval, err := s.ReviewCheck(ref); err == nil && eval.Approved && eval.Fresh {\n   832\t\t\t\tcontinue\n   833\t\t\t}\n   834\t\t\taction := \"seal and review \" + ref + \" (`pose review bundle \" + ref + \" --seal`), then `pose close \" + ref + \"`\"\n   835\t\t\tif !decision.Known {\n   836\t\t\t\taction = \"resolve \" + strings.Join(decision.Diagnostics, \"; \")\n   837\t\t\t}\n   838\t\t\towing = append(owing, CloseoutReviewOwing{Scope: ref, Decision: decision, Previous: \"unknown: no snapshot of the earlier policy or composition is recorded\", Action: action})\n   839\t\t}\n   840\t\treturn owing, nil\n   841\t}\n   842\t\n   843\tfunc mustScope(ref string) ScopeRef {\n   844\t\tscope, _ := ParseScopeRef(ref)\n   845\t\treturn scope\n   846\t}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
